WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
*upbeat synthesizer music*

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.000
We all know how it feels when you see someone across the room.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.060
Your eyes meet and everything slows down for a minute.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:21.240
Your medial prefrontal cortex plays a big role in your initial attraction to someone; and it judges physical attractiveness in just milliseconds.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.300
Does your heart flutter when you see your crush?

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:33.300
The adrenal gland causes the hormones adrenaline, epinephrine, and norepinephrine to be pumped through your blood. In turn, your heart beats more quickly.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.860
Or maybe you get butterflies in your stomach. This happens when adrenaline is sent to your lungs, muscles, and brain.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:46.800
This also directs blood flow to those areas, with not a lot of blood left for your stomach.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.400
Dopamine is also the reason you feel so good. 

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.880
Tests show, that love has a similar effect on dopamine levels as taking drugs, such as cocaine.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:06.060
Sometimes, we get obsessed or fixated on our new love. Couples who have recently fallen in love have significantly lower levels of serotonin.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:14.340
This is also experienced by people with Obsessive Compulsive Disorder, which may explain why you can't stop thinking about your new boo.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:22.800
Falling in love can be stressful, though. Cortisol, a stress hormone, is higher in people who have recently fallen in love...

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.480
...but overtime, kissing your loved one will lower your cortisol levels.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:35.220
One study even found, that being married will lengthen a man's life by 7 years and a woman's by 2 years.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:46.740
*upbeat synthesizer music*

